{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/the-world-below/1556016-interview-with-matthew-dawkins","id":1556016,"title":"Interview with Matthew Dawkins","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Earthbane Cycle","The World Below"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-11-30T17:37:57-08:00","id":1556016,"title":"Interview with Matthew Dawkins","content":"Matthew Dawkins is an author who has been involved in the creation of numerous fantastic supplements for the<i> World of Darkness, Chronicles of Darkness, Curseborne</i>, and other properties. He is also the party responsible for the well-liked <i>Gentleman Gamer</i> videos. Recently, we talked with him about his recent release in <i>The World Below</i>, a fantastic RPG setting in a vast underground kingdom. You can pick up a copy of the game here or here.<br /> <br /> <b><a href=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below.webp\" target=\"_blank\"><img alt=\"The World Below Cover Image\" width=\"232\" height=\"300\" data-align=\"none\" data-size=\"full\" loading=\"lazy\" border=\"0\" src=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below-232x300.webp\" data-fullsize-url=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below-232x300.webp\" data-thumb-url=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/the-world-below-232x300.webp\" data-title=\"Click on the image to see the original version\" data-caption=\"\" class=\"bbcode-attachment thumbnail js-lightbox bbcode-attachment--lightbox\" itemprop=\"image\"/></a></b><br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/interview-with-author-matthew-dawkins/\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.grimdarkmagazine.com/int...tthew-dawkins/</a><br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] So, can you give us a short rundown on what <i>The World Below</i> is all about?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] <i>The World Below</i> is a grand experiment in survival, community, and the slow-burn horror of a world that wishes you dead. It\u2019s a subterranean realm carved out over countless generations,<br /> equal parts wondrous and inimical. Think of it as a civilization in freefall \u2014 except everyone is climbing downward instead. You have monumental caverns the size of countries, predators that rewrite your anatomy if they bite you, and glittering resources that might save your people\u2026 or corrupt them entirely. It\u2019s about carving out a place in the dark and deciding together what kind of society deserves to survive.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What do adventurers \u201cdo\u201d in <i>The World Below</i>?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] They delve. They build. They bargain. They flee: occasionally with great dignity, often without it.<br /> <br /> Adventurers in <i>The World Below</i> are not murder-hobos; they are custodians of their lineage\u2019s future. They explore the labyrinthine depths seeking resources vital to their communities, negotiate with strange cultures, erect settlements, and confront a constantly shifting environment that refuses to behave itself. And when the caverns collapse or the flora and fauna is ambulatory and carnivorous, they\u2019re expected to handle that as well. It\u2019s very much a setting in which \u201cheroism\u201d is measured by survival and stewardship, not body count.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What sort of races are PCs allowed to be in </b><b><i>The World Below</i></b><b>? Do you have any sexy drow equivalents?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] We don\u2019t call them \u201craces\u201d because <i>The World Below</i> is not especially interested in the pseudo-essentialist fantasy taxonomy that says, \u201cHere\u2019s what dwarves are like and here\u2019s what elves are like.\u201d Instead, we use Dawns: your people, your upbringing, your cultural inheritance.<br /> If you <i>want</i> to play someone elegant, dangerous, and possessed of a certain subterranean allure, you absolutely can. But they\u2019ll be products of their environment and Dialectics rather than a drow clone with serial numbers filed off.<br /> <br /> Are there sexy people in the dark? Of course. Humans, serpentine entissia, furry makiru, and elegant elvkin always finds a way. But they\u2019re sexy because of confidence, mystery, and good cave-lighting.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What inspired you to make this setting?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] A fascination with civilizations under pressure. I\u2019ve long been drawn to stories of communities forced to adapt \u2014 ecologically, psychologically, morally \u2014 when the world becomes unrecognizable. I also adore subterranean environments and vertical horror: the majesty of a cavern lit only by bioluminescence, the oppressive silence you get two miles beneath the crust, danger above you and below.<br /> <br /> I wanted to explore how societies evolve when sunlight is a myth and every resource must be earned with blood, ingenuity, or diplomacy. And I wanted to create a fantasy world with consequences, where magic is powerful but mutagenic, where monsters are terrifying but ecologically plausible, and where hope is precious because it must be excavated.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What would you say the primary differences between </b><b><i>The World Below</i></b><b> and </b><b><i>Dungeons and Dragons</i></b><b>\u2019 Underdark are?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] The Underdark is, fundamentally, a dungeon writ large. It\u2019s a hazardous playground designed to challenge surface-dwelling adventurers. It\u2019s a place to visit.<br /> <br /> <i>The World Below</i> is a place to live. It is somebody\u2019s homeland, someone\u2019s ancestral tomb, someone else\u2019s holy pilgrimage site. Its cultures aren\u2019t evil by default, its monsters aren\u2019t XP dispensers, and the \u201cdarkness\u201d is not moral but environmental (well, sometimes it\u2019s thoroughly evil).<br /> <br /> Also, we don\u2019t assume omnipresent villainy or cartoonish malevolence. Our horrors have biologies, histories, and motivations. Our communities form alliances, rivalries, and trade networks. It\u2019s a functional ecosystem, not an alignment chart with stalactites.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What is a unique culture from </b><b><i>The World Below</i></b><b> that players might interact with?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] I\u2019m very fond of the Ipreceans, who are a community native to the World Below. They decided it was wisest to allow the refugees from the World Above in rather than locking the door and leaving them to the monsters of the Vast Underneath as huge stocks of food. They may be coming to regret this decision. The Ipreceans are a warrior-culture that clads itself in the remains of its enemies, its members masking themselves with insect skulls and the like. What lurks beneath all the layers of bone and leather is a being closer to a collection of leeches than a human being, as all Ipreceans steadily mutate into these semi-monstrous forms.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] Who is one of the antagonists that adventurers might end up facing?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] The Well Liches are those delvers who found the mystical Well of Kaos and tapped into its power. It changed them, and rather than spread sorcery evenly around the World Below for the betterment of all, they hoarded it and have yet to relinquish their power. In a mega campaign, I could well see the Well Liches as final boss material. But then comes the question of whether your player characters would make the same error they did. Would they distribute magic evenly, despite some communities being innately hostile and cruel, even cannibalistic? Would they choose to distribute power only to \u201cthe worthy\u201d? You can see how the Well Liches ended up the way they did.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What sort of unique mechanics exist in </b><b><i>The World Below</i></b><b>?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] As well as flexible spellcasting via Kaotic Sorcery and a mass of powers named Syntheses, I\u2019d go for Dialectics: how the environment and Kaos alter your character over time. Instead of leveling up in a vacuum, you evolve through tension: light vs. dark, tradition vs. mutation, survival vs. risk.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s also generational play, in which your character\u2019s deeds echo into the lineage that succeeds them. This reinforces the idea that you\u2019re not just delving for loot, but for the future of your people. And Storypath Ultra keeps all this brisk, character-driven, and cinematic rather than bogged down in tables.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] </b><b><i>The World Below</i></b><b> is a standalone RPG using the Storypath Ultra system but could you use it for other rules?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] Absolutely. I\u2019m a great supporter of larcenous creativity. If you want to use the setting with a different ruleset, you\u2019re not going to summon me like a gentlemanly Cenobite to chastise you.<br /> That said, Storypath Ultra meshes wonderfully with the tone of the game, and much of the setting\u2019s DNA \u2014 Dialectics, Kaos, generational advancement \u2014 is baked into the mechanics. You <i>can</i> port those ideas elsewhere, but Ultra does them natively and elegantly.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What is good viewing/reading/playing, media wise, for </b><b><i>The World Below</i></b><b>? What sort of shows or books or games capture its essence?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] I always suggest J.G. Ballard, so for a change I\u2019ll point to <i>The Descent</i> for claustrophobic tension, <i>Nausica\u00e4 of the Valley of the Wind</i> for ecological wonder mixed with dread, and <i>Morrowind</i> for its alien-yet-lived-in societies. China Mi\u00e9ville\u2019s novels \u2014 <i>Perdido Street Station</i>, <i>The Scar </i>\u2014 are also excellent inspiration for cultures shaped by hostile environments.<br /> <br /> And of course, documentaries on deep cave exploration are wonderfully grounding. Nothing reminds you of nature\u2019s indifference like watching a spelunker measure a crevasse with a plumb line while their helmet lamp flickers ominously.<br /> <br /> <b>[GDM] What can we expect from this series in the future?</b><br /> <br /> [MD] More settlements, ancestries, Callings, Dialectics, Sorceries, and more terrible revelations. We\u2019ve only scratched the surface \u2014 well, the <i>undersurface </i>\u2014 of what this world contains. Future supplements like <i>Secrets of Kalm &amp; Kaos</i> and <i>The Delver\u2019s Guide </i>will explore everything from new playable options and beast and pet taming, to expansive crafting rules and the deeper mysteries of Kaos.<br /> <br /> And, ideally, I want these future books to provide players opportunities to shape not just their communities, but entire epochs of the subterranean world. If <i>The World Below</i> is about anything, it\u2019s about carving meaning out of darkness. We intend to give you plenty more stone to carve.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83e\udd70","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-02-24T18:03:37-08:00","id":1558506,"title":"","content":"Really looking forward to At the Gates this fall.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Amethyst is my birthstone. She/they.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26996-amethyst","name":"Amethyst","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26996_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"873"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}